# Chapter 9 — The System That Produces Its Tomorrow

*An institution continues through the operations that make its continuation possible.*

Imagine a factory on a morning when wages have not arrived. The machinery works. There are orders to fill and materials in the yard. The workers know their jobs. Yet some cannot pay for transport, others must seek work elsewhere, and suppliers begin asking whether their own invoices will be honored. An interruption in payment starts changing which physical operations can take place.

Now imagine that the payment arrives. That event alone does not manufacture a meal, repair a machine, or complete a shift. It reconnects activities through which those things may happen. Workers obtain necessities, production resumes, customers receive goods, and revenue permits further commitments. The factory's ability to operate tomorrow is partly a product of what it does today.

The example is hypothetical. Its question is concrete: which operations regenerate the organization that makes those operations possible?

The preceding chapter followed competence distributed across people, tools, and practices. Its continuation depends on people being supported, equipment maintained, and learning carried through intervals before it yields a return. We now examine how economic claims organize those dependencies across a division of work. A payment can govern access to support, just as the sharing arrangements in chapter 7 can; the forms and mechanisms must be investigated in their own historical settings.

Autopoiesis gives this question its place in the book. In the biological formulation introduced in chapter 3, a network produces components that regenerate the network and constitute its unity.[1] The present chapter develops a social extension: economic activity continually renews capacities, obligations, and relationships through which further economic activity becomes possible. Its object is the production of an organization over time.

This supplies a missing distinction in the larger thesis. Disequilibrium provides the proposed physical driver. Assembly explains the formation of an organization and the possibilities it opens. Autopoiesis concerns the operations through which an established organization regenerates itself. These tasks belong to one account, but answering one does not automatically answer the others.

A machine can be assembled and then left to decay. A cell must continue producing conditions of its own living activity. An economic organization may replace employees, equipment, contracts, and products while reproducing a recognizable relation among them. To explain that continuity, we have to identify the work doing the regeneration. The continued existence of a name is insufficient.

The returning organization is what this chapter investigates as regenerative closure. Operations renew conditions of further operations, so that the network continues to produce its organization. Closure here concerns those dependencies. The system still exchanges energy, materials, and information with an environment. It can depend on inputs it does not produce while organizing the means through which it repeatedly obtains and uses them.

A broken connection need not end the organization if another operation can replace it. The decisive failure occurs when required renewal is lost without an adequate replacement. This gives the historical argument its form: an institution can survive a disappointed expectation, but it cannot continue indefinitely without the operations that reproduce its defining relationships.

Closure must be identified independently of the eventual outcome. We should name the required operations, observe what they regenerate, and specify what would compensate for their interruption. Otherwise calling a surviving system closed and a failed one open would merely rename the history we wanted to explain.

Begin with the movement through the factory. Production uses materials, energy, equipment, and people's activity. Sales can convert its output into monetary receipts. Payments give suppliers and workers claims they can use elsewhere. Some receipts support maintenance, replacement, training, and investment. If the relevant connections continue, another period of production becomes possible.

Several cycles overlap here. A household obtains and prepares food, supplies care, and supports people who participate in paid work. A supplier replaces its stocks and maintains its own equipment. A lender recognizes repayments and decides whether to extend another loan. The operations have different timescales, and any can be interrupted while others continue temporarily on reserves.

No firm produces all these conditions within its walls. The useful boundary may encompass a network of firms, households, public services, and financial operations. That boundary must be stated rather than expanded whenever the explanation encounters a difficulty. The organization remains open to energy and materials; its recurrent operations give those exchanges a particular form.

Marx's analysis of simple reproduction identifies a crucial feature of this process. Production renews more than a stock of commodities. It also renews the relation between people who control the means of production and people who must sell their labor-power to obtain a livelihood.[2] Participants can change while the relation continues. Their actions need not include an intention to reproduce capitalism as a whole.

Consider the worker's next return. Food, shelter, rest, care, and practical access to the workplace help make it possible. Receiving a wage can support those requirements while continued dependence on wages supplies a reason to return. The payment therefore participates in reproducing both a capacity and a relationship. Its significance exceeds a transfer between two accounts.

This is where jobs and affordable goods enter the argument. They help sustain participation in a productive cycle. Their importance cannot be reduced to persuading people that an institution deserves allegiance. A person may approve of the institution and lack the means to participate, or disapprove of it and depend on its next payment. Material dependence and political support are distinct consequences to investigate.

The same distinction applies to profits. A financial surplus may permit replacement and expansion, but it does not ensure that either occurs. Suppose, in another illustrative case, that an enterprise distributes cash while postponing essential maintenance. Its accounts can record success while the physical conditions of later production deteriorate. A theory of self-production must follow what gets regenerated, over what interval, and at whose expense.

Nor does this sketch assume that workers buy the entire output with their wages or that every sale automatically finances the next one. Investment, public expenditure, credit, trade, saving, and losses complicate the actual flows. The point is to trace the connections required for renewal, not to draw a closed monetary circle and declare the economy complete.

Recognized meanings are among those connections. A transfer is treated as a wage, loan, gift, or repayment through its relation to other operations. Luhmann makes communication central to social autopoiesis: communications connect to further communications that reproduce a social system.[3] The present argument follows those operations through their material dependencies, including what participants can eat, build, or enforce.

The synthesis proposed here brings Marx and Luhmann together through cybernetics. Marx makes the reproduction of material capacities and social relations central; Luhmann makes the reproduction of communicative operations central. Cybernetics connects these emphases by tracing how the consequences of activity return as information that changes subsequent activity. Ashby's account of disturbances and regulatory responses helps specify that connection.[4] The question becomes how material production and communicative organization continually condition and reproduce one another.

The formal and material aspects of the system now appear together. A contract specifies a relation among possible actions. Embodied practices make that relation effective: people keep records, recognize claims, transfer resources, and enforce decisions. Changing the relation can change which physical work occurs even while the equipment remains untouched. Calling the system jointly material and Platonic directs attention to both the realized operations and the relations constraining them.

Return to the unpaid wages. The contract establishes an obligation, but a missed payment changes whether people can travel to work or obtain necessities. Absences, complaints, interrupted deliveries, and production records make consequences available to other participants. Those communications may lead to a new payment schedule, a loan, collective action, or a change in management. If the response restores provisioning and production, it also helps sustain the people and institutions through which contracts are recorded, interpreted, and enforced. A formal relation directs material activity; material consequences enter communications that can preserve or revise the relation.

Marx, Luhmann, and cybernetics thus contribute to an account of one jointly material and Platonic system. Production sustains the operations through which its organization is recognized and changed; those operations direct the next round of production. Marx's social relations already belong within this formal organization, and communication itself requires material realization. The synthesis joins explanatory emphases within a continuing process. It does not divide society into a physical half and an independently operating realm of ideas.

Feedback contributes to autopoiesis when its effects help renew this network of operations. A manager may receive an accurate report and lack the resources to act on it. A successful correction may protect output while exhausting the workers or equipment needed for the next cycle. We must therefore trace both the response to a disturbance and the regeneration of the capacity to respond. Chapter 10 develops this reciprocal dependence: regulation sustains production, while production renews the means of regulation.

This also clarifies the role of ideology. People can explain their institutions as expressions of natural entitlement, sacred hierarchy, individual merit, or an unchangeable economic order. Such explanations can conceal the practices through which the institutions are continually made. Marx and Engels ground the production of ideas in material activity and social relations.[5] The investigation therefore begins with those activities and asks how their participants come to understand them.

An inaccurate explanation still has a material history and consequences. It can be taught, printed, repeated, enforced, and used to coordinate conduct. Its falsity concerns what it claims about the world. It does not make the acts performed through it disappear. In the proposed autopoietic account, ideology participates in reproducing relationships by shaping what people recognize, expect, and do.

Reification occurs when relationships produced through those activities appear as independently existing powers. Money seems to command resources by itself; a historically maintained hierarchy appears as a natural order. The materialist explanation follows the work, provisioning, enforcement, and recognition that make those powers effective. Ideological accounts can be false about their origin or necessity while the underlying relationships remain real. The formal relations belong within the materially realized system whose reproduction we are investigating.

We can now examine the author's compressed materialist argument: money comes from tax; taxes come from war; money exists to pay taxes; taxes exist for protection. This is materialism as an explanatory method: follow apparently abstract institutions into the resources they mobilize, the protection or coercion they organize, and the relationships those operations reproduce. Developing the argument requires a recurring process with specified historical conditions. The compressed formulation alone cannot establish a universal sequence of first inventions.

Imagine an authority trying to maintain a defensive force. It needs actual provisions, equipment, trained people, and transport. Some means of obtaining those resources must recur if the force is to continue. Successful protection can help productive activity continue; the capacity to compel contributions can also sustain the armed organization. Those are different routes through which its material support may be renewed.

Charles Tilly's account places war making and state making alongside the logic of protection rackets. His comparison, developed for European experience, challenges the assumption that organized protection must arise from a voluntary bargain.[6] The same power that offers security can produce insecurity. Reproduction can therefore depend on coercion as well as on a service that contributors value.

The book's next question is operational. Who gathers resources, who keeps records, who supplies the armed organization, and how does the resulting capacity make another round of contributions possible? A durable answer must trace people, goods, and enforceable obligations. The label protection cannot settle whether the people subject to the arrangement benefit from its continuation.

Taxation payable in a specified currency adds a monetary mechanism. In the chartalist account developed by writers such as Randall Wray, an enforceable obligation can create demand for the means accepted in settlement.[7] This explains a route by which a monetary claim acquires practical importance. It does not demonstrate that all money originated through taxation or that paying taxes exhausts the uses of money.

A simplified issuer illustrates the connection. An authority issues its currency in payment for supplies or work and requires obligations to be settled in that currency. Recipients can use it themselves or transfer it to others who need it. Settlement and renewed obligations can help sustain demand. In this arrangement, issuance must make currency available before it can be surrendered; the tax obligation does not itself manufacture the means of payment.

The material effect concerns the mobilization of resources. People supplying goods and work respond to claims that others recognize. Records and enforcement help preserve the arrangement through time. The same numerical token would not organize the same activities in the absence of those relationships. Here money's operation belongs to the self-production of an institutional network.

Actual monetary systems contain further processes. Commercial-bank lending can create deposits, as the Bank of England's account explains.[8] Repayment, settlement, regulation, and continued acceptance belong to the investigation. A universal story in which every monetary unit comes directly from a tax collection would miss the operations by which contemporary money is produced and used.

Protection also allows a provocative comparison: feudalism was a labor union. The common question concerns how contributions and reciprocal obligations sustain an organization that protects its participants. Fulbert of Chartres's letter of 1020 describes obligations of aid and fidelity between a lord and vassal, including reciprocal duties for the lord.[9] It gives us a specific statement to examine rather than a timeless definition of all medieval society.

The comparison has to preserve differences in membership and power. Relations among lords and vassals cannot simply stand for the condition of dependent peasants. Nor does an obligation of protection make hereditary lordship identical to collective organization by wage-earners. We should ask who can demand reciprocity, who controls the resources, and what happens to someone who refuses to contribute.

The historical thread worth following is the changing organization of labor and its protections. A guarantee can mean a declared obligation, a practically enforceable claim, or an expectation that circumstances usually fulfill. These must be separated. Fulbert gives us a statement of reciprocal duty. Demonstrating its delivery would require evidence about what people actually did when protection or assistance was demanded.

Within an agrarian organization, the proposed loop connects access to land, work, surplus, organized force, and continuing claims. Its participants occupy unequal positions and need different things from its repetition. The chapter's hypothesis is that feudal relations declined where their regenerative closure broke: the operations sustaining those relations ceased to renew one another, and effective replacements no longer reproduced the same organization.

That hypothesis requires a particular region, period, and set of relations. A lord's loss of military importance, a change in access to labor, or a producer's new route to a livelihood would interrupt different connections. Each might prompt adaptation within the existing order or contribute to its transformation. We cannot decide which occurred simply by announcing that a promise of protection had been broken.

England's Ordinance of Laborers of 1349 provides a concrete view of an attempt to preserve a relation under changed material conditions. Its preamble describes deaths from pestilence, scarce labor, and demands for higher wages. Its provisions seek to compel eligible people into service, restrict departure, and hold remuneration to earlier levels.[10] The language presents the problem from the authority's position; it is not a neutral description of workers' conduct.

The material relationship had become harder to reproduce on its previous terms. The ordinance attempted to make enforcement compensate for that difficulty. Its existence shows an effort to restore the old conditions of service, not that the effort succeeded everywhere. The disturbance and the response therefore give us separate things to investigate: which relationships had weakened, how force was used to sustain them, and what alternatives workers could actually pursue.

This is more precise than dating the end of feudalism to the plague or to one law. Waged service already appears within the medieval document. Different forms of labor could coexist, and a later wage economy would still require law, provisioning, and enforcement. A transition changes the relations through which these activities connect; it does not create labor or dependence for the first time.

The industrial comparison consequently begins with the renewal of a livelihood through wages. An individual can be formally able to change employer while lacking the reserves to remain without work. Workers who organize collectively try to change the terms on which that dependence is reproduced. The capacity to bargain then requires its own continuing organization.

The British Act of 1824 repealing laws concerning combinations of workmen illustrates the contested legal conditions of that organization. Its provisions addressed combinations over wages and working arrangements; the opening also retained punishment for specified attempts to deter work.[11] The relationship between collective capacity and legal protection was being reconstructed through particular rules, not settled by a declaration of unrestricted freedom.

The Webbs define trade unionism around continuing association among wage-earners to maintain or improve their working conditions.[12] Labor unions, in the functional sense developed here, are mutual protection pacts: members pool resources and organize action to defend conditions they cannot reliably secure alone. For the present argument, that protection becomes a process to reconstruct. Consider an illustrative union in which contributions support representation, records, and reserves. Those capacities permit collective action; the results can sustain membership and future contributions. If members cannot afford dues, cannot act together, or cease to recognize the organization's decisions, that cycle can weaken.

The material content of union protection is visible in the House of Lords debate of May 1, 1871. The speaker described sickness, burial, and some superannuation funds, alongside assistance moving members to places where work was available. He also discussed legal obstacles to protecting union property and recovering funds.[13] These were claims made in advocacy of legislation, but they identify concrete operations to investigate.

The assistance with movement is especially revealing. Knowing where a job exists does not get an unemployed worker there. A reserve that pays for travel can reconnect a person to paid work; renewed earnings may then support further contributions. The guarantee acquires force through records, coordination, money, and transport. If those operations disappear, the promise alone cannot move anyone.

The same questions apply to modern labor organization: what protection is undertaken, which members can claim it, and what recurring contributions and capacities make delivery possible? Collective bargaining, a reserve, or recognized representation can each affect different parts of that process. Their significance lies in how they change the material conditions of continued participation.

A failed guarantee is therefore one possible route to the loss of regenerative closure. Members might withdraw contributions when support fails, weakening the ability to supply future support. They might instead rebuild the reserve, change the leadership, obtain external assistance, or sustain the organization through a reduced service. The result has to be traced. Closure can be repaired, or its replacement can reproduce a different institution.

A lordship, a state, and a union have different constituents, powers, and purposes. Their comparison becomes useful when it identifies what their operations regenerate. It cannot rest on calling each a protection pact. The relevant similarity is a returning relationship between organized capacity and the activities that maintain it; the differences determine how that relationship works and whose position it preserves.

The book's corresponding hypothesis is that capitalism will remain the dominant system for as long as it can keep delivering jobs and affordable goods. Growing markets can sustain a material promise of accessible work and inexpensive provision. Where that promise is broadly delivered, participation renews both people's capacities and the relationships through which production proceeds. The system's continued dominance would then depend on its practical performance as well as on the stories told about it. Its effect on allegiance is one part of the explanation; the larger claim concerns how material delivery reproduces the organization itself.

Employment and affordability are not guaranteed by the name of the system. For a particular account we must identify whose work is available, what income commands, and whether food, housing, care, and other requirements can actually be obtained. A lower price for one manufactured product cannot by itself establish that the conditions of participating in the wider economy have improved.

This suggests a prediction rather than an assurance of permanent dominance. If an arrangement persistently undermines the capacities required for its renewal, compensating operations must appear or the arrangement must contract, transform, or fail. Credit, public provision, unpaid care, migration, or coercion might sustain some connections for a time. The explanation must identify the actual compensation and its limits; it cannot infer a hidden repair simply because the system has survived.

Autopoiesis can also preserve an obstacle to development. An organization may reproduce the claims of its participants by restricting an activity that would expand productive capacity elsewhere. In a hypothetical housing system, for example, falling prices might make shelter more accessible while weakening the collateral on which existing lending depends. The houses remain, but the renewal of financial claims and the provision of shelter can pull in different directions.

The same problem can arise within production. A firm may cut expenditure on training or care to protect its immediate accounts while weakening the labor and knowledge on which the wider network depends. The unit that benefits and the unit whose reproduction deteriorates need not be the same. A system boundary is therefore part of the explanation, not a line drawn after success has been identified.

This is a demanding case for the energetic hypothesis. Self-production explains how an organization can persist, including how it can defend constraints that support its own continuity. The proposed tendency toward maximum energy extraction and entropy-production rates must additionally explain when such constraints are transformed. We cannot infer that every surviving institution realizes the widest available maximum.

Disequilibrium remains the proposed driver of that larger development. Economic arrangements organize access to materials, energy, equipment, and human effort. Their recurrent operations can sustain an initially costly experiment, preserve an assembly through an unproductive interval, and retain its results for later work. They can also prevent those activities. The energetic account must show which changes in these operations make a new physical pathway more likely.

An autopoietic explanation should consequently make interruptions informative. In the opening example, would a delayed payment break production because workers lack transport, because the transfer is not recognized as valid, or because suppliers withdraw? Would a reserve preserve the cycle? Could another organization perform the missing operation? These questions distinguish a regenerative dependency from the observation that events happened repeatedly.

Historical interruptions can test these dependencies if the records explain why exposure differed and rule out relevant alternative causes. A payment failure caused by an already collapsing business cannot, without further identification, establish that payment failure initiated the collapse. Compare interruptions, pre-existing reserves, substitute providers, and the timing of operational changes. A surviving institution with the proposed indispensable operation demonstrably absent would challenge the specified dependency; merely relabeling every survivor as closed would evade the test.

They also distinguish the survival of people from the preservation of a particular institution. A change of ownership, a different arrangement of public provision, or a new form of collective control may preserve useful capacities while altering the relations regenerated through them. To evaluate such a transition, we must specify what is retained, what changes, and how the work continues during the change.

The connection to dialectical transformation lies here. An arrangement develops capacities through a particular organization. Its continued reproduction can then obstruct the use or renewal of those capacities. A successor must find a practical way to preserve what works while changing the relationships that have become a barrier. This is a proposed pattern for investigation, not a promise that every contradiction produces a successful successor.

The factory can now be seen as part of a system producing the conditions of its next day. Payments, provisioning, learning, enforcement, and shared representations help make that day possible. Ideology can obscure those operations by presenting their outcomes as powers belonging naturally to money or property. Material analysis makes the operations visible; autopoiesis asks how they regenerate their organization.

One of the capacities such a system can reproduce is inquiry. It can train investigators, maintain instruments, preserve results, and support work whose return has not yet arrived. These operations develop the material support proposed in chapter 4. They also depend on participants who can interpret a signal, compare an outcome with an expectation, and revise what they do. The next chapter examines how those regulatory capacities are assembled, including what changes when a mind can model its own activity. Keeping an economic organization going and explaining a cognitive mechanism remain distinct tasks within the same proposed physical history.

