1. Varela, Maturana, and Uribe, [“Autopoiesis: The Organization of Living Systems, Its Characterization and a Model”](https://doi.org/10.1016/0303-2647(74)90031-8) (1974); see [S33](sources.md#s33). The social account developed here extends the biological comparison and identifies operations and boundaries rather than treating every persistent object as autopoietic.

2. Marx, *Capital*, volume I, [chapter 23, “Simple Reproduction”](https://www.marxists.org/archive/marx/works/1867-c1/ch23.htm), especially its concluding account of reproduction of the capitalist relation; see [S09](sources.md#s09). The factory and interruption scenarios are original illustrations, not reported case studies.

3. Luhmann, [*Social Systems*](https://uberty.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/08/Niklas_Luhmann_Social_Systems.pdf), English translation (1995), especially the communication discussion on printed pages 137–142; see [S10](sources.md#s10). The chapter's combined attention to communication, bodily dependencies, and class relations is its own proposal. Prior work connecting these traditions is acknowledged under [S60](sources.md#s60).

4. Ashby, [*An Introduction to Cybernetics*](https://www.ashby.info/Ashby-Introduction-to-Cybernetics.pdf) (1956), Part III; see [S45](sources.md#s45). Regulation alone is not presented as sufficient for autopoiesis.

5. Marx and Engels, [*The German Ideology*, Part I.A](https://www.marxists.org/archive/marx/works/1845/german-ideology/ch01a.htm), especially the discussion of material activity and consciousness; see [S82](sources.md#s82). The treatment of commodity relations also draws on [*Capital*, chapter 1, section 4](https://www.marxists.org/archive/marx/works/1867-c1/ch01.htm), under [S09](sources.md#s09). The chapter's account of ideology within self-production is an interpretation.

6. Tilly, [“War Making and State Making as Organized Crime”](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511628283.008), in *Bringing the State Back In* (1985), 169–191; see [S79](sources.md#s79). The accessible opening supports the bounded protection-racket comparison. The operational questions and hypothetical defense cycle are the book's construction, not a reconstruction of one medieval state.

7. Wray, [“What Are Taxes For? The MMT Approach”](https://www.levyinstitute.org/blog/what-are-taxes-for-the-mmt-approach/) (2014); see [S80](sources.md#s80). The text identifies the chartalist mechanism and uses a simplified issuing authority. It does not generalize the financing arrangements of a currency issuer to every government or establish a universal origin of money.

8. McLeay, Radia, and Thomas, [“Money creation in the modern economy”](https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/quarterly-bulletin/2014/q1/money-creation-in-the-modern-economy) (2014); see [S11](sources.md#s11).

9. Fulbert of Chartres, [“On Feudal Obligations”](https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/fulbert1.asp) (1020), translated in University of Pennsylvania *Translations and Reprints* (1898), vol. 4, no. 3, 23–24; see [S81](sources.md#s81). This normative statement concerns lords and vassals; it does not establish that obligations were always fulfilled or that peasants participated on equivalent terms.

10. [Ordinance of Laborers, 1349](https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/seth/ordinance-labourers.asp), translated text in the Internet History Sourcebooks; see [S84](sources.md#s84). Used for the authority's stated response to mortality and labor scarcity, not as evidence that its provisions were uniformly enforced or that one shock ended feudalism.

11. [An Act to repeal the Laws relative to the Combination of Workmen; and for other Purposes therein mentioned](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo4/5/95/pdfs/ukpga_18240095_en.pdf), 5 Geo. IV c. 95 (1824); see [S85](sources.md#s85). The opening and repeal provisions support this bounded legislative example, not a complete history of union legality.

12. Sidney and Beatrice Webb, [*The History of Trade Unionism*, chapter I](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/66887/66887-h/66887-h.htm), revised edition extended to 1920, originally published 1894; see [S83](sources.md#s83). The membership-and-reserves cycle is an original illustration. The comparison with lordship is functional and bounded, not an identity or a claim of direct institutional descent.

